---
title: Read the marsh route
lang: en
---

## Read the route

By the end of this lesson, you can identify a station on the route sketch and distinguish a station name from a signal instruction.

Lantern Marsh is an invented place. Its keepers carry coloured shutters between reed islands. Tonight you join keeper Mira on the west route. The stations are Sedge Gate, Moth Bend and Last Reed. A name tells you where a record belongs. A signal tells the next keeper what happened there.

The [route ledger]{The shared notebook that records station visits in route order.} stays at Sedge Gate. Write the station name before adding a signal.

--- Follow the sketch

## Follow the sketch

Start at Sedge Gate and follow the boardwalk to Moth Bend. Last Reed sits beyond the fork. The dotted line is a return path used when the low boardwalk closes.

/assets/route.svg
alt: Route sketch showing Sedge Gate, Moth Bend and Last Reed joined by a boardwalk, with a dotted return path.
caption: The three stations on the west route.

### Outward route
as: accordion

Visit Sedge Gate before Moth Bend. Check the station plate whenever two paths meet.

### Return route

Use the dotted path when the low boardwalk closes. Record the change in the route ledger.

close: accordion

--- Name or instruction

## Name or instruction

Mira writes "Moth Bend, hold the amber shutter" in her pocket book. The words before the comma identify the station. The remaining words tell the keeper what to do.

### Which words identify the station?
as: choice
scored: false

(x) Moth Bend
feedback: Moth Bend is the name on the station plate.
( ) Hold the amber shutter
feedback: This is an instruction, not a station name.
( ) Pocket book
feedback: The pocket book holds the record.

--- Make a pocket map

## Make a pocket map

Draw the west route on paper. Put each station name beside its mark, then add a second line for the return path. Keep your drawing for the route practice later in this course.

Ask another learner to point to Moth Bend using only your drawing. If they hesitate, note which mark or label caused the hesitation. Revise that part of the map. There is no answer key for this drawing.

Before leaving, write one question you would ask Mira about a path that is not on the sketch.
